{
  "dom_id": "step-q330353",
  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Suppose ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are both solutions of the homogeneous system\n${x}^{\u2032}(t)=A\\text{\u00a0}x(t).$\nWhich of the following is **always** a solution of the same system?\n\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(t)\\cdot {x}_{2}(t)$\n- $x(t)={x}_{1}(t)\u2299{x}_{2}(t)$ (element-wise product)",
  "worked_solution": "The *linearity principle* for systems of linear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) states that if ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous linear system, then for any constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ the linear combination \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}{x}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis also a solution of the system. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given expressions.\n\n- Option I is a solution to the system. Indeed, notice that\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t)\n$$\n\nis a linear combination of the solutions with constant multiplies ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ respectively.\n\n- Option II might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$ Notice that the multiplier of the second term is not a constant.\n\n- Option III might be *not* a solution to the system since it's not a linear combination of the solutions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\".",
